---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How this site is built"
  description="Astro, Azure Static Web Apps, Bicep and GitHub Actions — the whole pipeline, including the bits that were more awkward than the docs suggest."
>
  <h1>How this site is built</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Astro on Azure Static Web Apps, provisioned with Bicep and deployed by GitHub Actions.
    The whole thing is static — no server, no adapter, and no client-side JavaScript on a
    content page.
  </p>

  <h2>The stack, briefly</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Choice</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Site generator</td><td>Astro 7, static output</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Content</td><td>MDX in content collections, Zod-typed frontmatter</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Styling</td><td>One hand-written stylesheet, CSS custom properties</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hosting</td><td>Azure Static Web Apps, Free tier</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Infrastructure</td><td>Bicep, one resource</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pipeline</td><td>GitHub Actions, with PR preview environments</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Content is typed, not trusted</h2>
  <p>
    Both collections — <code>posts</code> and <code>projects</code> — share a Zod schema for
    title, description, date, tags and draft status, with projects adding a status enum and
    optional repo/live links. A typo in frontmatter fails the build rather than quietly
    rendering an empty <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, which is the entire point.
  </p>
  <p>
    Drafts are visible in <code>astro dev</code> and excluded from a production build. There's
    an escape hatch — a <code>BUILD_DRAFTS</code> environment variable — so the templates can
    be exercised by a real build before anything is published.
  </p>

  <h2>Zero JavaScript, deliberately</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing here needs to be interactive, so nothing here ships a runtime. Two consequences
    worth noting:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Dark mode has no toggle.</strong> It follows
      <code>prefers-color-scheme</code>, because the alternative is a script, a storage
      read, and a flash of the wrong theme on first paint. A toggle is a real feature; it
      just isn't a free one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Syntax highlighting is build-time.</strong> Shiki emits both themes as CSS
      variables and a media query picks one. The highlighter never reaches the browser.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Diagrams</h2>
  <p>
    Mermaid isn't wired up yet, and that's a decision rather than an oversight. Both routes
    cost something:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>rehype-mermaid</strong> runs the real Mermaid inside a headless Chromium at
      build time. Perfect fidelity, but it puts a browser download in the CI pipeline.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A pure-JS renderer</strong> such as beautiful-mermaid emits SVG with no DOM
      at all. Fast, but it's a reimplementation, so the long tail of Mermaid syntax is a
      question mark.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Either produces inline SVG with no client JS, which is the requirement. The choice gets
    made when there's a post that actually needs a diagram, and the diagram will show which
    one is right.
  </p>

  <h2>Infrastructure</h2>
  <p>
    One Bicep file, one resource: a <code>Microsoft.Web/staticSites</code> on the Free tier
    with <code>provider: 'Custom'</code> and staging environments enabled. <code>Custom</code>
    matters — it stops Azure trying to generate its own workflow file and wire itself to the
    repository. The pipeline owns the deployment; Bicep owns the resource.
  </p>

  <h2>The pipeline, and the Oryx detour</h2>
  <p>
    The default Static Web Apps workflow hands your repository to Oryx, Azure's build
    engine, and lets it work out how to build the site. That's convenient right up until
    you want to control the Node version, run a type check, or know why a build failed.
  </p>
  <p>
    So this pipeline doesn't use it. GitHub Actions checks out, installs on a pinned Node,
    runs <code>astro check</code>, runs <code>astro build</code>, and then the deploy action
    runs with <code>skip_app_build: true</code> — it uploads <code>dist</code> and nothing
    else.
  </p>

  <Callout type="tip" title="The gotcha">
    With <code>skip_app_build</code>, <code>app_location</code> stops meaning "where the
    source is" and starts meaning "where the built output is". And because only
    <code>dist</code> gets uploaded, <code>staticwebapp.config.json</code> has to be inside
    it — so it lives in <code>public/</code> and Astro copies it across. Leave it at the
    repository root and it's silently ignored: no error, no headers, just a site that scores
    badly on a security scan for no visible reason.
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Pull requests get their own preview environment on a stable URL, and a second job tears
    it down when the PR closes. The Free tier allows three at once, which is more than
    enough for one person.
  </p>

  <h2>Headers and caching</h2>
  <p>
    <code>staticwebapp.config.json</code> sets a restrictive CSP, HSTS, <code>nosniff</code>,
    a referrer policy and a <code>Permissions-Policy</code> that switches off hardware APIs
    this site will never ask for. Astro's hashed assets under <code>/_astro/</code> get a
    year of immutable caching; HTML gets <code>must-revalidate</code>, so a deploy is visible
    immediately.
  </p>
  <p>
    The CSP still carries <code>'unsafe-inline'</code> for styles, because Astro's scoped
    component styles are inlined. Removing it means nonces or hashes, which means
    server-side rendering — a real trade against "static only". It stays for now, and it's
    worth being honest that it's there.
  </p>

  <h2>What's deliberately missing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No analytics.</li>
    <li>No comments.</li>
    <li>No web fonts — the system stack loads instantly and looks fine.</li>
    <li>
      No interactive islands. There's room for a self-contained Blazor WebAssembly demo as
      an exhibit later, which is exactly the kind of thing an island is for. It isn't built
      yet.
    </li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
